# Citation metadata for OntoBoard.
#
# Format:       Citation File Format (CFF), schema version 1.2.0
# Schema guide: https://github.com/citation-file-format/citation-file-format/blob/main/schema-guide.md
# Schema:       https://github.com/citation-file-format/citation-file-format/blob/main/schema.json
#
# 1.2.0 is the current version: it is the latest release of the citation-file-format
# repository (tag 1.2.0, published 2021-08-09) and the schema pins the key with
# pattern ^1\.2\.0$, so "cff-version" below is not a value you may bump freely.
#
# Validate before committing. Both of these exit non-zero on a bad file:
#   pipx run cffconvert --validate -i CITATION.cff
#   gem install cff && ruby -rcff -e 'CFF::File.validate!("CITATION.cff")'
#
# TWO THINGS ARE DELIBERATELY MISSING. Please fill them in.
#
#   1. orcid. Add it to the author entry below, in the full URI form the schema demands:
#          orcid: "https://orcid.org/XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX"   # TODO Ebrahim: your ORCID
#      The key is absent rather than present-with-a-placeholder, and the reason is not
#      the obvious one. The schema pattern is
#      https://orcid\.org/[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{3}[0-9X]{1}
#      and "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000" MATCHES it. An all-zeros placeholder
#      would therefore pass every validator, go green in CI, and feed a false identifier
#      into the APA and BibTeX snippets GitHub generates and into anything that harvests
#      them. A false ORCID that validates is worse than no ORCID, so there is none here.
#      The XXXX form above is used deliberately: it does NOT match the pattern, so if it
#      is ever uncommented and left unfilled the validator fails loudly instead of
#      shipping a lie quietly.
#
#   2. email. Same reasoning: omitted, not invented. If you want a contact address in the
#      published citation, add it to the author entry:
#          email: "XXXX"                                    # TODO Ebrahim: your address
#      XXXX fails the schema's email pattern on purpose, for the reason given above.
#      Note that the repository already publishes TWO DIFFERENT addresses for you, at
#      NOTICE line 5 (one domain) and at paper/main.tex:42 and
#      paper/ontoboard-iswc2026.tex:33 (another). They are not repeated here, because this
#      file is published metadata and a comment is not the place to add a third copy.
#      codemeta.json beside this file asserts NEITHER, on purpose, so that the two agree.
#      Pick ONE canonical citable address and make NOTICE, both papers and codemeta.json
#      agree before publishing. Do not use a personal address here; this file is published
#      metadata that Zenodo, Software Heritage and GitHub all harvest.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
title: OntoBoard
abstract: >-
  OntoBoard is a Protégé Desktop plugin, packaged as an OSGi bundle, for maintaining
  OBO-style ontologies with the Ontology Development Kit (ODK) and ROBOT. It embeds
  robot-core as a library rather than shelling out to the ROBOT command-line tool, so
  ROBOT operations such as report, measure, profile, explain, SPARQL, transform,
  template, term export, IRI renaming and import-module extraction run in process
  against the ontology already open in the editor. OntoBoard also scaffolds and builds
  ODK projects, running most generated build targets with no make, shell or container
  installed; compares two releases axiom by axiom; edits ROBOT template spreadsheets;
  ships a built-in library of ontology design patterns; and draws a canvas of classes,
  individuals and their relations read from the asserted axioms, with reasoner
  conclusions and axiom-level explanations available on demand.
version: "1.120.0"
date-released: "2026-10-14"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ebrahimnorouzi/ontoboard"
url: "https://ebrahimnorouzi.github.io/ontoboard/"
authors:
  - given-names: Ebrahim
    family-names: Norouzi
    affiliation: "Information Service Engineering (ISE), FIZ Karlsruhe - Leibniz Institute for Information Infrastructure, Germany"
keywords:
  - ontology engineering
  - ROBOT
  - ROBOT templates
  - ODK
  - Protégé plugin
  - OWL
  - OBO
  - knowledge graph
  - spreadsheet

# NO "preferred-citation" KEY, ON PURPOSE.
#
# preferred-citation redirects credit away from the software and onto another work.
# The ISWC paper in paper/ is not published, so there is no other work to redirect to.
# A stub would technically validate (a reference needs only authors, title and type),
# but GitHub renders preferred-citation in place of the software metadata: the APA and
# BibTeX snippets under "Cite this repository" would then advertise a paper nobody can
# find, and would stop showing the version, date and licence of the thing being cited.
# Omitting it means GitHub cites the software, which is the honest answer today.
#
# When the paper is accepted, add a block like this and change "message" to say that the
# paper is the preferred citation:
#
#   preferred-citation:
#     type: conference-paper
#     title: "<paper title>"
#     authors:
#       - given-names: Ebrahim
#         family-names: Norouzi
#         affiliation: "Information Service Engineering (ISE), FIZ Karlsruhe - Leibniz Institute for Information Infrastructure, Germany"
#     collection-title: "<proceedings title>"
#     year: <year>
#     doi: "<doi>"
#
# Also: add "identifiers:" with a Zenodo DOI once the repository is archived there.
# A DOI is what makes the citation resolvable; the GitHub URL alone is not.
